// @no-instantiate
// TODO(miscompile): two closure arms whose PARAMETER object types are field-permuted check
// clean (`vl check --codegen` rc 0) and emit a module that does not instantiate.
//
// FOUND BY REVIEW, NOT BY A SWEEP. It surfaced while a reviewer was checking whether an
// unrelated change altered the closure-signature tag partition for permuted object params —
// the shape turned out to be broken on master already, in both directions, so it is filed here
// rather than attributed to that change (which was closed for a different, real defect).
//
// THE TWO PARAMETER TYPES ARE ONE TYPE. `{a: i32, b: string}` and `{b: string, a: i32}` are
// structurally equivalent under the 2026-08 ruling, and that ruling now reaches the emitter's
// struct rows (`types/permuted-named-alias-shares-row.vl`). So `(PA) => i32` and `(PB) => i32`
// are one SIGNATURE, and a union of the two is a union with one distinct arm — which is
// presumably what the lowering is not expecting.
//
// The failure is a nullability mismatch on the box, not a tag mismatch:
// `expected (ref null $type), found (ref $type)`. That points at the union-box carrier rather
// than at the signature tag table, so the fix is unlikely to be in `cloSigTagSlot`.
//
// The directive is bare: the two engines that run this corpus word the rejection differently
// and share no substring, so this pins the FACT (the module does not instantiate) and leaves
// the wording to the engine.
//
// THE PERMUTATION IS INCIDENTAL — RE-DERIVED, and this is the sharper statement of the
// defect. The trigger is a union whose two arms are ONE TYPE, and the permutation is only
// the least obvious way to write that. Three probes place it:
//
//   · `FA = (PA) => i32` beside `FB = (PB) => string` (permuted params, DISTINCT
//     signatures) — compiles and runs, printing `A isFA`. So permuted PARAMETER types are
//     not the problem;
//   · a permuted param on a SINGLE-arm annotation (no union at all) — compiles and runs;
//   · `type FA = (i32) => i32` beside `type FB = (i32) => i32`, no objects and no
//     permutation anywhere — `vl check` rc 0, the module VALIDATES, it runs, exit 0, and it
//     PRINTS NOTHING. Both `is` tests should be true.
//
// SO THE DEGENERATE FORM IS A STRICTLY WORSE TIER THAN THIS FILE, and it is pre-existing on
// master (verified against a master-seed compiler, not inferred). A valid module that
// silently answers NO to every arm of a one-distinct-arm union is a wrong VALUE, not a
// refused module — the failure class `@no-instantiate` cannot pin and `@trap` explicitly
// rejects. It is left here as a note rather than a second fixture because inventing a
// fixture KIND for it is a decision this file should not make on its own; whoever closes
// this should file it properly and fix both together.
//
// The likely shape of the fix follows from that: `x is FA` over a union whose arms collapse
// to one signature has no distinguishing tag to compare, so the tag comparison is against a
// value the box never carries. The nullability mismatch this file's own module reports is
// the same collapse arriving one layer down, at the box carrier.
//
// When it closes, flip to `@run` with `@log A isFA` / `@log B isFB` — under the ruling BOTH
// calls should report BOTH arms, since the two signatures are the same type.
type PA = { a: i32, b: string }
type PB = { b: string, a: i32 }
type FA = (PA) => i32
type FB = (PB) => i32

function pm(lbl: string, x: FA | FB) {
  if x is FA { print(lbl + " isFA") }
  if x is FB { print(lbl + " isFB") }
}

function fa(v: PA) { v.a }
function fb(v: PB) { v.a }

pm("A", fa)
pm("B", fb)
